00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:16.230
If you ask the US military how many bases
they have overseas, you won’t really get

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.230
an answer.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.800
They don’t make it all too hard to find
out about the larger ones—Ramstein Air Base

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.840
in Germany, Thule Air Base in Greenland, Camp
Hansen in Japan.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.250
These all show up on the closest thing to
an official catalogue of the US military's

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:32.930
real estate there is—the annual Department
of Defence Base Structure Report.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:38.329
According to this document the American military
has some 514 sites outside of its borders,

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:41.870
but, there are some noticeable omissions to
this list.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.831
For example, the US has a rather secretive
drone base in central Niger, however, according

00:00:46.831 --> 00:00:48.930
to this list, it doesn’t exist.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:54.860
The US has more than ten sites in Syria, however,
according to this list, they don’t exist.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.110
The US has a satellite surveillance facility
in Australia’s Northern Territory so well

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:04.280
known, in fact, that it has a whole fictional
TV show based on it, but, according to this

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.729
list, it doesn’t exist.

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:10.899
In fact, according to this list, there are
just four defense department installations

00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:15.969
in Africa—a base in Djibouti, a joint British-American
base on Ascension Island, an NSA site in Kenya,

00:01:15.969 --> 00:01:19.649
and a Naval Medical Research facility in Egypt.

00:01:19.649 --> 00:01:24.170
Of course, if you dig a little deeper into
the vast archive of unclassified military

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:30.409
documents, you find this—a slide from a
presentation clearly showing 34 US military

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:31.970
sites in Africa.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:37.619
With omissions such as these, one can assume
that that total 514 number is far from the

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:42.619
real count of how many facilities the US military
maintains abroad.

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:46.179
Part of this could be attributed to the fact
that it’s sometimes tough to define what

00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:48.490
a military base is.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.399
Again looking at the African continent, the
only site that looks like what most would

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:57.200
traditionally think of as an overseas military
base is Camp Lemonnier in Djibouti.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.100
It is the only permanent, exclusive US military
site, at least according to their own definition,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.049
on the continent, hosts about 4,000 members
of the US military at a time, and is the primary

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:09.679
base of operations for the US Africa Command.

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:14.530
You see, the US military splits the world
into six regions each with their own infrastructure

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:15.829
of bases.

00:02:15.829 --> 00:02:17.600
Each has a hierarchy of sites.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.150
The highest, in the case of Africa Command,
are those permanent, full-blown bases—the

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.879
one in Djibouti and the one on Ascension Island.

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:28.959
One step below that are what are called Cooperative
Security Locations.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:32.760
These are, according to the US military’s
definition, “host-nation [facilities] with

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.360
little or no permanent U.S. personnel presence,
which may contain pre-positioned equipment

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.220
and/or logistical arrangements and serve both
for security cooperation activities and contingency

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.220
access.”

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.069
CSL’s are useful to the US military because
they are much less flashy and less permanent—they

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:52.849
don’t require the same kind of political
capital as to set up as a full-size base like

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:54.379
the one in Djibouti.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:58.840
Bases are often unpopular and receive press
scrutiny, both in the US and the host country,

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.470
so small, few-hundred person CSL’s have
the advantage of being able to be set up with,

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:04.950
essentially, no publicity.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.370
You can think of them as smaller versions
of the kind of bases you find in Djibouti

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:14.790
or Ascension island which can, rather quickly,
become bigger bases should the need arise.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:19.480
The remaining twenty known sites on the continent
are what are called contingency locations.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:24.400
Now, this terminology can be used for a lot
of different types of facilities, but, in

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.130
essence, what it means is that these are temporary
sites established as part of ongoing missions.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:35.040
For example, the contingency location in Garoua,
Cameroon was set up for the Americans to provide

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.560
logistics and intelligence support in the
Cameroonian’s fight against Boko Haram.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.879
What that actually means, though, when you
break through the military’s PR language,

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:45.469
is that this is a drone base.

00:03:45.469 --> 00:03:49.530
Unlike other American drone bases, it’s
relatively easy to find info about the one

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:54.079
in Garoua perhaps because it’s primarily
home to surveillance drones, rather than strike

00:03:54.079 --> 00:03:55.079
drones.

00:03:55.079 --> 00:03:59.400
For other contingency locations, though, it
is much less clear what exactly their purposes

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.219
are and for some, they aren’t even publicly
acknowledged.

00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:08.090
For many, the US military just has small agreements
with foreign governments and the general public

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.230
gets very little info at all.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:16.220
So, the final, real answer for how many US
bases there are abroad is that we don’t

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.220
know.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.160
If you define every military installation
as a base, compiling all publicly available

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.170
information, one set of research reached a
number of 800.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:29.190
Of course, the real number could be something
far different from that but as the general

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.470
public, there’s just no real way to know.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:37.030
But the next question that arises about the
US’ overseas presence is why?

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.600
In the era of nuclear weapons that can obliterate
any city on earth in an hour, aircraft carriers

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.470
sailing worldwide with more aircraft than
some country’s air forces, and airplanes

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:50.040
that could land troops in any country on earth
in a day, why does the US bother spending

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:54.840
so much money maintaining bases in allied
countries during peacetime?

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:00.660
The primary reason has to do with a military
concept known as the loss of strength gradient.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.350
This concept essentially theorizes that, the
further a conflict is away from a military’s

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:10.040
home country, the less military power that
nation is able to bring to the fight.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.470
This is largely because it is, of course,
complicated and expensive to bring troops

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:17.340
and equipment over long distances.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.790
The book that originally defined this loss
of strength gradient proposed that the way

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:25.340
to counteract this effect was to establish
bases outside of a country’s home territory

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:30.020
since these can help reduce the effective
distances to conflict and, therefore, it’s

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.400
easier to bring more power to the fight.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.300
The US has certainly taken this concept to
heart and has put quite a lot of work into

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.870
trying to flatten out their loss of strength
gradient.

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:44.280
That is to say, they want to make it just
as likely that the US would win a war in east

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.380
Asia as North America.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:51.360
As an example of how these bases aid that
mission, much of the operations of the US’

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:57.130
wars in Afghanistan and Iraq were conducted
here—at Ramstein Air Base in Germany.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:01.880
This base and the other surrounding US military
facilities in the Rhineland-Palatinate state

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:06.410
make up the largest grouping of American service
members in the world and one of the largest

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.910
groupings of Americans anywhere outside the
US.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:14.040
The city that Ramstein and many of the other
facilities are in is home to only about 100,000

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.760
full time residents, however, the American
bases are staffed by more than 50,000 personnel

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.720
at any given time.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:25.800
This makes Ramstein Air Base like a small
American city in Europe.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.670
It has outposts of plenty of American restaurant
chains that you won’t find anywhere else

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.810
in Germany—Johnny Rockets, Chili’s, PF
Chang’s—in addition to an American-style

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.630
department and grocery store.

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:39.900
It has an American post office, an American
high school, four baseball diamonds, two American

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:45.310
football fields, American suburban style housing,
and even campuses of four American universities—University

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:50.820
of Maryland, Oklahoma, Central Texas College,
and Embry Riddle Aeronautical University.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.290
Quite a lot of work is put into making sure
that Ramstein is as similar to any base in

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:59.880
the US as possible—both in terms of lifestyle
and capability.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.050
One central role for Ramstein and other US
bases in Europe during the wars in Iraq and

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:09.530
Afghanistan was as a stopover point for personnel
and cargo en route to combat.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.911
Ramstein’s convenient location, less than
a seven hour flight from all of the middle

00:07:12.911 --> 00:07:17.930
east, where many of the US’ recent military
operations have been, makes it a pivotal logistics

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:22.660
hub since it would be far more complicated
to fly personnel and cargo nonstop to theatre

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:26.730
over the more than eleven hour flight from
the continental US to the Middle East.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:31.250
Still today, with less US presence in the
middle east, Ramstein plays a central role

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.080
in getting US military members to Europe.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.260
There are regular flights, typically about
twice a week, from Baltimore to Ramstein in

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:42.450
addition to a number of regular flights from
stateside military bases like Wright-Patterson

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:47.410
Air Force Base in Ohio, Dover Air Force Base
in Delaware, and McGuire Air Force Base in

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:48.410
New Jersey.

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:52.140
These are not flights on commercial airlines
but rather charter flights available only

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:57.970
to members of the military operated by charter
companies like Atlas Air and Omni Air International.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:02.680
Beyond its role as a logistics hub, Ramstein’s
geographic position plays a critical role

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.120
in the US’ use of drones in the Middle East.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:09.670
You see, American drones are communicated
with by satellite but, due to the distance

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:13.750
between the Middle East and Creech Air Force
Base in Nevada, where the communications from

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:17.730
the different drone piloting sites across
the US are centralized, a single satellite

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.740
could not convey information from Creech to
the Middle East.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:25.040
That’s just because there’s too much curvature
in the earth for a satellite at a reasonable

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.290
orbit altitude to have line-of-sight with
both areas.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:32.409
They could have one satellite relay info to
another, but this would significantly increase

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:37.250
the time it would take for the signal to travel
from Creech to the drone and, when piloting

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:42.210
and attacking remotely, one needs as close
to real-time communications as possible.

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:47.200
Therefore, the signals travel by fiber optic,
transatlantic cable from the US to Ramstein

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:51.490
where a relay station then sends the signal
up to a satellite based over the area that

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:54.330
can communicate with America’s drones in
the Middle East.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:58.320
Without Ramstein, these drones would not be
nearly as capable.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.480
Beyond convenience and capability, another
major reason for America’s heavy overseas

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.300
military presence is power projection.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:10.090
This is a term used by militaries that refers
to, according to the US Department of Defense’s

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:14.790
definition, “the ability of a nation to
apply all or some of its elements of national

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:15.790
power—political, economic, international,
or military—to rapidly and effectively deploy

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:16.790
and sustain forces in and from multiple dispersed
locations to respond to crises, to contribute

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:17.790
to deterrence, and to enhance regional stability.”

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.960
In this context, it’s essentially how fast
a country can get to the fight, if a fight

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.290
should arise.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.930
Power projection is as much an offensive power
as a defensive one.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:29.250
It’s about making sure that every other
country in the world knows that America can

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:34.500
and potentially will respond to whatever they
decide is a threat in a timely manner.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.990
According to the US Department of Defense,
the four countries that currently present

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:43.540
the greatest potential national threat to
the US are Iran, Russia, China, and North

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:44.540
Korea.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:48.560
Looking at the global map of bases, it’s
no coincidence that the greatest concentrations

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:53.620
of overseas bases are near Russia’s population
center in the east, in the Middle East, and

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:54.650
in East Asia.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:10:00.240
Meanwhile, there’s relatively little US
military presence in South America, Africa,

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.710
South and Southeastern Asia, and Australia
since there are fewer threats to the US in

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:05.710
these areas.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:11.390
Still, though, the US military has a nearly
permanent presence on every continent.

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:16.350
Even on Antarctica, where by international
treaty militarization is banned, the US military

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:20.800
skirts this regulation by dealing with the
logistics of supplying American research bases,

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.640
which is allowed by the treaty.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:26.690
Some might characterize this experience with
Antarctic operations as, “convenient,”

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.490
in the event of any future conflict in this
region.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:34.620
While the US’ network of overseas bases
in only a part of its overall power projection

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:39.020
mission, which also includes its nuclear weapons,
aircraft carriers, submarines, and more, the

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:43.230
main messaging they convey is that the US
can get to anywhere fast.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:50.180
But, predictably, these bases are controversial—both
at home in the US and abroad.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:56.400
As one example, this is the island of Okinawa,
Japan and this is the land used by the US

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:57.400
military.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:02.660
On this dense island of 1.5 million, 26,000
US service members man these sites.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:06.890
While the Japanese government is supportive
of the US presence in Okinawa and elsewhere

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:12.650
in Japan, locally, there have been decades
of tensions between Okinawans and the US military.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:17.380
The US bases there have been an economic,
social, and environmental burden on the island

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:21.680
as, while the US military’s presence in
Japan as a whole is viewed largely as a benefit

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.260
for the country, Okinawans are the ones that
have to put up with having a large proportion

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:29.700
of their home under the control of a foreign
military.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.480
Okinawans reportedly feel like they’re being
ignored by mainland Japan and they’ve therefore

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:38.840
been protesting, particularly against a forthcoming
base move to a new site on the island, for

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:39.840
years.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:43.990
This is the story for pretty much every country
that hosts US military bases—they’re often

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:48.120
considered by foreign governments as a benefit
for the country as a whole since it give them

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:52.310
an essence of protection by perhaps the most
powerful military in the world, but it comes

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:56.120
at a burden to the communities the bases are
physically located in.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:01.050
In Okinawa, while the bases do provide a decent
amount of employment for locals, it’s now

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:04.460
thought that the island could be better off
economically with the land that these bases

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:07.110
take up being used for commercial purposes.

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:12.140
Back in the US, some believe that their tax
dollars are being used to defend other countries.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:16.790
Some consider these overseas bases antiquated
in the era of international military alliances

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:21.700
like NATO, extensive aviation infrastructure
that can get US forces anywhere on earth in

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:25.480
a matter of hours, and the deterrent threat
of nuclear weapons.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:30.550
Meanwhile, others would argue that they are
crucial assets to US diplomacy and power projection.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:35.650
They would argue that their very existence
maintains the US’ superpower status.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:40.941
This is all to say, simply, that the US military’s
worldwide presence is controversial… but

00:12:40.941 --> 00:12:42.970
likely effective.

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:47.780
They certainly do make the US military seem
more formidable in the international eye which

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:54.220
many Americans would consider a positive,
but the final, grand question is at what cost?

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:59.000
With the cost in dollars, the cost in geopolitical
tensions, the cost in community detriment,

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:05.960
the simple cost in how the world views the
United States as a country, is it worth it?

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:09.930
If you’re a viewer of Wendover Productions
you probably are someone who likes learning

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:11.610
about interesting things.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:14.990
One topic that I personally think is quite
interesting that I haven’t touched much

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.620
before on this channel is probability.

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:21.410
The field of probability is one that seems
simple at first but becomes more and more

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:26.800
complex with the more you learn and can have
some quite useful real-world applications.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:31.430
If you want to learn how to apply the principles
of probability, Brilliant has five fantastic

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:36.240
courses taking you all the way through the
basic to advanced concepts in probability.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:40.899
Like all their courses, these do an amazing
job of breaking down complex subjects into

00:13:40.899 --> 00:13:43.950
intuitive, simple chunks and then bringing
those all together.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:49.500
Of course, these are just a few of Brilliant’s
